+## [2.26.0] — 2026-09-29
+
+All three sidecars (faces, NSFW, seekbar) now work on another machine — tokens, path mapping, uploads under proxy size caps. Face scanning is 20-30x faster on CPU, the dashboard is lighter and faster on phones, backups and seekbar previews are more reliable, and the README has real screenshots.
+
+### Security
+- Guest sessions received the admin statistics push over WebSocket (including cluster peer stats); each connection now gets the payload for its own role, like `GET /api/stats`.
+- **Sidecar API tokens are write-only in the dashboard.** `GET /api/config` returns `apiTokenSet` instead of the token and the raw-config view redacts it — this also covers `advanced.seekbar.apiToken` and the faces sidecar's `advanced.ai.faces.sidecarToken`, which were returned in clear.
+- **seekbar-service 0.4.0:** with `SEEKBAR_API_TOKEN` set, `/sprite/{id}` and `/meta/{id}` need the token too, so a sidecar exposed through a tunnel doesn't hand out thumbnails of your videos (`SEEKBAR_PUBLIC_MEDIA=true` keeps them open). Both sidecars log a warning when they listen beyond localhost without a token.
+- **seekbar-service 0.4.0:** optional `SEEKBAR_ALLOW_ROOTS` limits path mode to the listed directories (symlinks resolved); a path outside them is answered "source not found", so the app uploads the file instead.
+
+### Performance
+- **Tailwind is now a prebuilt static stylesheet.** The dashboard, login, add-account and setup pages loaded the Tailwind Play CDN (`cdn.tailwindcss.com`), which compiled CSS in the browser on every load and re-scanned the DOM on every mutation — a constant CPU/RAM cost on phones with big galleries. `npm run build:css` (Tailwind v3 CLI, config in `scripts/tailwind.config.cjs`, same `tg-*` colours and v3 defaults as the old inline config) now emits `src/web/public/css/tailwind.css`, which is committed so Docker and bare-metal installs need no build step. It is linked after `main.css` (where the CDN injected its `<style>`), so computed styles are identical to the CDN build. The service worker precaches it, and `tests/tailwind-css.test.js` fails CI when the committed CSS is stale.
+- **No more request floods from WebSocket events.** The Engine card refetched `/api/monitor/status` on every `history_progress` / `download_*` / `queue_length` event, and the status bar + gallery footer refetched `/api/stats` (and monitor status) on every `download_complete` / `file_deleted` — dozens of requests per second during a backfill or auto-prune sweep. They now render from the server's existing pushes (`monitor_status_push` every 3 s, debounced `stats_update`), refetching only on WS reconnect.
+- **Hidden pages stop rendering.** The Queue page rebuilt its chips (via `innerHTML`, re-attaching listeners), totals and sparkline — and re-sorted the whole job list on every new job — every 150 ms even while another page was open; now only the bottom-nav badge updates until you come back. Chip counts update in place, and finished/failed jobs are capped at the newest 500. The Logs view only appends lines while visible, and the Cluster page's 30 s peer poll stops when you leave it.
+- **Faster startup.** The `<link rel="modulepreload">` hints hard-coded `?v=2.5.1`, and since the server's version stamper skips URLs that already carry a query, the 7 preloads fetched module URLs nothing ever imported; they are now bare so the server stamps them like the `<script>` tag. `settings.js` (~3.3k lines) and `backfill.js` are no longer in the boot import graph — they load on first visit (and are warmed in idle time right after boot). The first route renders as soon as auth + groups are known instead of also waiting on `/api/stats` and the cluster-peer lookup (still awaited when a non-local gallery scope is saved).
+- **The always-visible monitor status dot no longer repaints every frame.** Its "running" pulse (and the Engine card's ping) animated `box-shadow`, which forces a paint per frame for as long as the monitor runs; they now animate `transform` / `opacity` on a `::after` ring (compositor-only) with the same look, and respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.
+- **Gallery DOM stays bounded on 10k–100k-file libraries.** The gallery keeps a sliding window of tiles (≈400 on phones) over the loaded list: rows scrolled far away are replaced by a spacer of the exact measured height and re-rendered from memory when you scroll back — no refetch, no jump. Cuts always land on row starts (column count + time-section headers), so tiles never shift columns.
+- **WebSocket storms tamed, dead phone sockets dropped.** Some events were written to every client as fast as they were produced: `download_progress` once per gramJS chunk, `history_progress` once per scanned message, and `queue_length` and `queue_changed` once per enqueue. There was no ping/pong either, so a phone whose connection went half-open kept 5–15 MB queued for about 15 minutes. These events are now coalesced in 500 ms windows (latest wins per download, scan or queue; at most 2 progress frames per second per download). Message shapes are unchanged, so already-open tabs keep working. Held frames always go out before the messages that depend on them: a job's progress before its start, complete, error or cancel, a scan's progress before its cancelling or done, and everything before `monitor_state` or a global queue operation. A client more than 1 MiB behind skips progress frames only. If it would miss a state change (a download finishing, a deletion, a job completing), it is disconnected instead, and the dashboard reconnects and re-syncs. A 30 s ping terminates sockets that miss a pong or stay backed up across two pings; browsers answer pings automatically, and the SPA reconnects on its own. A simulated 10 s burst (20 downloads, a backfill scan, 2000 enqueues) went from 19,020 frames (3.4 MB) per client to 500 frames (107 KB). WebSocket protocol errors no longer surface as an uncaught exception.
+- **Thumbnail generation no longer starves file I/O.** Up to 16 sharp jobs ran at once, but each holds a libuv pool thread for its whole run and the pool had Node's default of 4 threads. During a gallery scroll, every `stat`, `sendFile` read and hash queued behind thumbnails: a probe read's p50 latency rose to 470 ms. The Docker image, `runner.js`, `runner.sh` and the PM2 ecosystem file now set `UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE=16`, and the default image-thumb concurrency is 4 (`THUMBS_IMG_CONCURRENCY`). Thumbnail throughput is unchanged, and the same probe now runs at 0.6 ms p50 during a 480-thumb burst. `express.static` also no longer runs a `stat()` for `/api/*` and `/files/*` requests, which never map to a file under `public/`.
+- **Avatars without a photo no longer hit Telegram on every render.** Each sidebar re-render requested `/api/groups/:id/photo` for chats without a cached photo, and every request asked every account again to resolve the chat. The server now remembers "no photo set" for 1 h. It remembers a chat for 10 min only when every connected account reports it as not found. FLOOD_WAIT, timeouts and connection errors are never cached. Repeat requests get an immediate 404, and the 404 response headers are unchanged. **Refresh photos**, the group-name refresh and saving a group always ask Telegram again. A chat that adds a photo appears within the hour, or immediately after **Refresh photos**.
+- **Thumbnail scrolling after the first hour no longer waits on the server.** `/api/thumbs/:id` was sent with `public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate`, so once a tile was more than an hour old, every tile in a scroll waited on a 304 round-trip. It now sends `private, max-age=3600, stale-while-revalidate=2592000`. It stays fresh for the same hour, so a regenerated thumbnail still shows up within the hour; after that the cached tile renders at once while it revalidates in the background. `private` keeps shared proxies from storing auth-gated images.
+- **HTTP compression is now actually on.** The server only loaded the `compression` middleware if the package happened to be installed, and it was never listed in `package.json`, so responses went out uncompressed. It is now a regular dependency. The SPA's text assets drop from about 2.1 MiB to 0.5 MiB gzipped, and gallery page JSON shrinks more than 10×. Raw file routes (`/files/`, `/share/`, `/photos/`, the cluster file bridge), Range requests and media types are never compressed, so video seeking still works. `COMPRESSION_LEVEL=0` now turns compression off.
+- **Sidebar and Downloads load without waiting on Telegram.** `/api/groups` and `/api/downloads` used to wait for a fresh Telegram dialogs fetch every time the 5-minute name cache expired. That meant one `getDialogs` per account, run one after another, plus up to 60 s of gramJS flood-sleep. A failing account also made every request try again. The name cache is now stale-while-revalidate: an expired map is returned immediately while one shared background refresh runs, and the accounts are queried in parallel. A failed or empty refresh keeps the previous names and is retried at most every 30 s. With nothing cached yet (first boot), a request waits at most 2 s before rendering config and DB names.
+- **New `downloads` indexes.** `idx_group_name_size` covers the sidebar's per-group count/size/name aggregate (about 2× faster at 150k rows, index-only). `idx_gallery_type_pinned_date` and `idx_gallery_group_pinned_date` serve "pinned first" gallery pages straight from the index; a type tab with pinned-first dropped from 25 ms to 0.1 ms per page at 150k rows. Existing libraries over 50k rows get these indexes, plus `idx_file_path` (below), built in the background after the dashboard is listening. The builds start 90 s after boot and run one at a time 45 s apart, so an upgrade never delays startup or the healthcheck. That is about 0.4 s in total at 150k rows, roughly linear with size. Queries run on the old plans until the builds finish. If the process is killed in the middle of a build (for example a healthcheck restart loop on a very large library on a slow disk), that index is not retried automatically. The log names it and gives the command to build it while the dashboard is stopped: `npm run build-indexes`, or `node scripts/build-indexes.js` in Docker. Smaller and new libraries build them at startup. No existing index is removed.
+- **One aggregate pass per sidebar paint instead of four.** `/api/groups` and `/api/downloads` each ran their own `GROUP BY group_id` over every row, and the auto-triggered `POST /api/groups/refresh-info` added a `DISTINCT` scan plus a per-group `UPDATE` walk. The per-group name/count/size aggregate is now computed once and cached for 15 s. The cache is invalidated by the same events that refresh the footer stats (download complete, deletes, purges, config changes) and by `groups_refreshed`. Refresh-info reuses the cached aggregate, and its per-group `UPDATE` seeks `idx_group_name_size` (200 groups: 50 ms down to 10 ms at 150k rows).
+- **Gallery bulk delete no longer freezes the server.** Selected paths were resolved with a `REPLACE(file_path, …)` comparison, which scanned the whole table once per path: about 7–14 s of blocked event loop for 1000 tiles at 150k rows. Paths now resolve through a new `idx_file_path` index, taking 5 ms for 1000 paths. The SPA sends tile ids, and paths remain supported for older clients and peer tiles. The same index speeds up the 404 auto-prune in `/files/` and backup file lookups (15 ms full scan down to an index seek).
+
+### Fixed
+- **Update check could miss app releases.** It asked GitHub for the repo's "latest release", which can be a sidecar release (`faces-v…`, `nsfw-v…`, `seekbar-v…`); it now picks the newest `vX.Y.Z` app release. Sidecar release workflows no longer mark their releases as Latest.
+- **Backup mirror "Run now" failed with "This database connection is busy".** The catch-up walk held a SQLite cursor open while queueing upload jobs on the same connection. It now walks the library in batches of 500 and yields between them, so a large library no longer stalls the dashboard either.
+- **A backup job whose local file is gone no longer errors the whole destination.** A missing or unreadable file (deleted, rotated away) now fails that one job immediately instead of retrying five times and then flagging the destination as errored. A successful mirror "Run now" also clears a stale error badge.
+- **Snapshot backups: retention now actually keeps "Retain copies" archives.** Pruning ran on a 60 s timer instead of after the upload, logged failed deletes as "pruned", and could touch any file under `snapshots/`. It now runs right after each snapshot upload, only considers `snapshots/snapshot-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS.tar.gz`, logs listed / kept / pruned counts honestly, and resets the destination's Files / Size to what is left on the remote. The local staging copy in `data/backups/` is deleted once uploaded (never while another destination still has it queued). Manual-mode destinations are unchanged. Staging archives left behind by earlier versions are not touched — delete old `data/backups/snapshot-*.tar.gz` files by hand if you want the space back.
+- **Scheduled snapshots ran twice per slot.** The cron check ticks every 30 s and a matching minute contains two ticks, so a `0 3 * * *` destination built and uploaded two archives each night (halving what "Retain copies" covered). It now fires once per matching minute.
+- **Editing a backup destination wiped its saved credentials.** The Edit form says "leave blank to keep" but the server replaced the whole provider config, so saving any change (or clicking Test connection in the form) dropped every secret you didn't retype. Blank secrets now keep their stored values, and the form pre-fills the non-secret fields (host, bucket, paths, …) instead of showing them empty. If an earlier edit already wiped a destination's secrets, re-enter them once.
+- **Wrong page count with "Pinned" on in a group's gallery.** The per-group total ignored the pinned filter, so pagination kept offering pages of nothing.
+- **Finished maintenance jobs could still look "running".** Status endpoints for re-index, thumbnail build and video optimise laid the last progress tick over the job's own state, so a stale `stage` / `running` from progress could win. The job's own `running` / `stage` / `error` now always take precedence, and a finished job's saved progress no longer carries `running: true`.
+- **Docker: DNS failures (`EAI_AGAIN`) on hosts that mount `/etc/hosts` / `/etc/resolv.conf` as `0640`.** The app runs as `node` and couldn't read them, so every lookup failed. The entrypoint now makes `/etc/hosts`, `/etc/resolv.conf` and `/etc/hostname` world-readable (they hold no secrets) before dropping privileges.
+- **"Disk Quota Exceeded" on every download, even after deleting files.** The downloader's disk-usage counter only ever went up, so once an install had downloaded `maxTotalSize` (default 50 GB) over its lifetime, every download failed — deletes and auto-rotate didn't help. The quota check now also reads the catalogue's total size (once a minute) and pulls the counter down to it, so deleting or rotating files lets downloads resume. A file shared by several downloads (duplicate detection) still counts once for new downloads. No action needed.
+- **Video optimise failed on iPhone videos with metadata tracks.** MP4/MOV files carrying a data track (Apple `mebx` timed metadata, timecode) failed the faststart rewrite with "Could not find tag for codec" and were retried on every sweep. The rewrite now retries once without data tracks — audio, video and subtitles are kept; the non-playable metadata track is dropped. A file that still fails three times is left alone (shown under "Skipped") until it changes on disk, and a failed rewrite no longer leaves a `.faststart.tmp` file next to the video.
+- **Seekbar: an external sidecar never really worked.** The URL and token entered in the dashboard were ignored (only `SEEKBAR_SIDECAR_URL` was read); a sidecar that couldn't see a video silently left it to the app's own ffmpeg; and when it wrote sprites to its own folder the database recorded that remote path, so the player's hover preview got a 404 while the video counted as done. Sprites now always end up in the app's `data/seekbar` (downloaded back when needed) — if that fails the video is retried by the next scan instead of being recorded. A remote sidecar that is down at startup is re-checked every 30 s instead of staying "unavailable" until a settings save or restart.
+- **Seekbar sidecar: JPEG sprites always failed, and a deleted sprite was never regenerated** while its JSON was still there (the sidecar answered "done" without writing anything). Fixed in seekbar-service 0.4.0; the app also avoids the second case with 0.3.3.
+- **NSFW external classifier:** video tiles were sent to the sidecar as temp-file paths it can't read on another host, and the first video switched every later photo from path mode to base64 for the rest of the session — tiles are now sent from memory. A photo the sidecar allowed but couldn't find was stored with no score instead of being sent to it. Behind a reverse proxy with a path prefix, a dropped connection aborted instead of retrying (the liveness check went to `https://host/health` instead of `https://host/nsfw/health`).
+- **Seekbar previews for long videos were marked "failed" for good.** The seekbar sidecar call gave up after 60 s while the sidecar kept encoding, the app then started a second, local ffmpeg on the same video, and that one was killed after 120 s with an error the scan treats as a broken file — so long videos never got a preview. Jobs are now submitted to the sidecar and polled until done, with a time budget of the video's length (5–60 min); once the sidecar accepted a job no local ffmpeg is started for it. Running out of time, or the sidecar forgetting the job (restart), is retried by the next scan instead of marking the video failed. Works with the current sidecar (seekbar-v0.3.3), no update needed. Existing "failed" marks are cleared once on upgrade, so the next seekbar scan retries those videos (files that really are broken are marked again).
+- **Failed backfills stayed in memory forever.** Finished and cancelled backfill jobs are dropped from the in-memory job list after the 5-minute grace window, but failed ones never were. A group whose auto catch-up kept failing added one entry per attempt for the life of the process. Failed jobs are now evicted the same way; they remain in the persisted history the Backfill page reads.
+- **Deleting one file could drop another group's gallery entry.** `DELETE /api/file` removed every DB row with the same file name, so deleting `photo_123.jpg` in one chat also removed another chat's row for its own `photo_123.jpg`, even though that file stayed on disk. It now removes only the rows for that exact path. The SPA also sends the tile's id, which the server checks against the real file first. With an id, only that download is removed, and the file stays while another download (download-time dedup) still uses it. Gallery bulk delete now sends the id of every selected tile that shows the same file, not just one of them.
+- **Gallery tiles popped in blank and the next page loaded late.** The tile-thumbnail and load-more `IntersectionObserver`s (gallery + Maintenance → Thumbnails) used the viewport as root while `#content-area` is the scroller, so their 1200–1500 px prefetch margins were effectively 0. They are now rooted on the scroller, and a short page re-checks the sentinel so paging can't stall.
+- **Scrolling back up after a long scroll left the top of the gallery empty.** The old DOM trimmer measured the spacer as the sum of tile heights (≈3× too tall with 3 columns), removed tile counts that weren't a multiple of the column count (tiles shifted columns), never re-rendered trimmed tiles, and stopped observing new tiles after a trim.
+- **Deleting a file shifted the viewer by one.** After a `file_deleted` push (auto-prune / rescue sweep) or an in-viewer delete, later tiles kept their old `data-index`, so tapping them opened the neighbouring file. Tiles are now re-indexed in place, and an in-viewer delete no longer re-renders (and scrolls) the whole gallery.
+- **AI / similar search results grew the previous view's pages underneath them** when scrolled to the bottom.
+- **Returning to the Library threw the loaded gallery away.** Switching to Settings / Queue / … and back emptied the grid and refetched page 1, losing the scroll position (and any type filter). The grid, filter and scroll position are now kept when nothing that shapes the list (chat, type filter, pinned, cluster scope) changed; pull-to-refresh and purges still refetch. Other pages now open at the top instead of at the gallery's scroll offset.
+- **Phones: bottom UI slid under the browser toolbar / home indicator.** The layout used `100vh` (the *largest* mobile viewport); body, Queue list, log pane, sheets and the gallery-scope menu now use `dvh` with a `vh` fallback. `viewport-fit=cover` is set, so the existing `env(safe-area-inset-*)` rules finally take effect (they were all 0): the bottom nav grows by the home-indicator inset instead of squeezing its icons, the viewer's top bar / bottom controls, sheets, sidebar drawer and side notches (landscape) are padded, and on an installed iPhone PWA in light theme the white status-bar text gets a dark band instead of vanishing on the white header. The browser/status-bar `theme-color` follows the app theme (was a fixed brand blue).
+- **360 px layouts.** The video control row (430 px wide) hides skip ±/mute below `sm`; the gallery selection bar (under the bottom nav, off-screen) and the Queue batch bar sit above the bottom nav + status bar, wrap and go icon-only; the FAB no longer overlaps the status bar or a selection bar; Maintenance page headers no longer overflow horizontally; the Group Settings modal fits the visible screen with only its body scrolling.
+- **Tap targets.** Viewer Share / Delete / Download are ≥ 44×40 px, the Group Settings close button 40 px, Queue row buttons 36 px with more spacing, sheet close buttons 44 px, and bottom sheets can be swiped down from a finger-sized area around the grab handle. On touch screens the mini-player controls are always visible and unpinned tiles no longer carry an invisible, tappable pin button.
+- **Login / add-account zoomed in on iPhone** when focusing an input (15 px text); inputs are 16 px, and `min-height: 100dvh` is no longer overridden by the `100vh` fallback.
+- **Viewer swipes / drag-to-close were dead on phones**: `touch-action: manipulation` let the browser claim the pan and cancel the pointer stream. The media panes (image, video tap layer, audio, office/fallback) now own their gestures; text / code / PDF panes keep native scrolling.
+- **Pull-to-refresh never fired on touch** — it listened to pointer events on a scrolling container, which the browser cancels once it starts panning. It now uses passive touch events (mouse keeps the pointer path).
+- **Video buffering spinner sat over smooth playback.** It showed on `stalled` (fires during healthy progressive download) and only hid on canplay/playing. It now tracks `waiting` → cleared by `playing` or `currentTime` advancing, shows at once only on a cold start, waits 200 ms for mid-playback waits/seeks, and never shows while paused.
+- **Double-tap to seek also paused the video / toggled fullscreen** on touch: the second tap's click is swallowed and `dblclick` is ignored without a hover-capable pointer.
+- **Deleting from the viewer could delete-then-hide the wrong file.** `file_deleted` is broadcast before the HTTP response returns, so the index-based splice removed the neighbour too. The file is now removed by identity and the viewer advances to the next item instead of closing.
+- **Empty gap in the viewer.** The never-populated preview strip reserved a 72 px band between the media and the info bar.
+- **One tap on a sidebar chat could open it several times / send several monitor-toggle PUTs.** Click + keydown handlers were re-attached to every row on each sidebar render, but rows keep their DOM when nothing changed, so after N `config_updated` events a tap ran N times. The list now uses one delegated listener, the ▶/⏸ toggle ignores repeat taps while its PUT is in flight, and Enter/Space on the row's inner buttons no longer also opens the chat.
+- **Saving a chat's settings on the Groups page no longer reloads the list.** It re-fetched every chat from Telegram, blanked the list, jumped back to the top and dropped the search filter; now only that row updates.
+
+### Added
+- **faces-service 0.5.0 is now the bundled face sidecar** (20-30x faster on CPU, see its changelog). Bare-metal installs download it on the next start; the old binary stays on disk. Embeddings are compatible, so existing People groups are kept.
+- **Back closes the open overlay.** Android's system Back, the browser Back button and the iOS PWA edge swipe now close the media viewer, a bottom sheet or the Group Settings modal (topmost first) instead of leaving the page underneath. Closing an overlay with ✕ / Esc / swipe consumes its history entry, so the next Back still goes to the previous page; navigating from an overlay (e.g. Group Settings → Backfill shortcut, FAB → Browse chats) waits for that step so it can't be undone.
+- **Pinch and double-tap zoom in the image viewer** (touch / pen), with panning while zoomed; the mouse-wheel zoom now zooms toward the cursor. Swipe-to-navigate and drag-down-to-close are paused while zoomed or pinching.
+- **Pin / unpin from the viewer's action bar** (admins, local files) — same endpoint as the tile's pin chip; the gallery tile updates in place.
+- **Run the NSFW and seekbar sidecars on another machine** — a GPU box, a separate container, behind a reverse proxy (path prefixes like `https://host/seekbar` work) or a Cloudflare Tunnel. Maintenance → NSFW → External and Maintenance → Seekbar previews → System health → External now take the URL, an optional **API token** and an optional **path mapping** (`app path=sidecar path`, for a shared mount at a different path). **Test** reports whether the sidecar is reachable, its version, whether the token is accepted and how files will reach it. When the sidecar can't read the app's files, NSFW images are uploaded to it (anything over 1.5 MB is downscaled to 1024 px first) and seekbar videos are uploaded in 32 MB chunks with the finished sprite downloaded back — both stay under Cloudflare's 100 MB request limit. Every field has an env var that wins over the dashboard (`TGDL_NSFW_SIDECAR_URL` / `TGDL_NSFW_API_TOKEN` / `TGDL_NSFW_PATH_MAP`, `SEEKBAR_SIDECAR_URL` / `SEEKBAR_API_TOKEN` / `SEEKBAR_PATH_MAP`), and changes apply without a restart. Setup with copy-paste Docker commands: docs/DEPLOY.md "Running a sidecar on another machine". Local and auto-spawned sidecars work exactly as before.
+- Sidecar updates that go with it: **nsfw-service 1.2.0** (optional `TGDL_NSFW_API_TOKEN`, raw image uploads) and **seekbar-service 0.4.0** (video uploads, the app's sprite settings applied per job, `ghcr.io/botnick/tgdl-seekbar` image). Older sidecars keep working: nsfw-service 1.1.0 gets base64 images and no token; seekbar-service 0.3.3 is used for videos it can read (same path or a path mapping) and the app's own ffmpeg renders the rest, as today. New installs download seekbar-service 0.4.0; an already-downloaded sidecar binary is kept.
+- **Face clustering with a sidecar on another machine: path mapping, token and Test in the dashboard.** Maintenance → AI → External now takes the API token (write-only) and a path mapping next to the URL (`advanced.ai.faces.pathMap` / `TGDL_FACES_PATH_MAP`), so a GPU box that mounts the downloads at a different path reads photos and videos in place instead of receiving every file. **Test** reports the version, whether the token is accepted and how files will be sent. Files it can't read go to faces-service 0.5.1's new raw `/detect/upload` (a quarter less data than base64; older sidecars keep getting base64). Requests now stay inside a ~40 MB body budget: a photo that used to hit a proxy's limit (a 168 MB PNG failed with 413 behind a 100 MB cap) is sent as a 4096 px copy with its face boxes scaled back, and video frames are grouped by size. The token is sent as `X-API-Token` instead of `Authorization: Bearer`, so it no longer clashes with a reverse proxy's own auth. Local and Docker-compose sidecars are unchanged; the pinned sidecar binary stays 0.5.0.
